@@@@ hands-on-c-reverse-list-then-cycle
situation: hands-on
topic: Linked lists in C
options: {"strategy":"code"}
voice: wordy
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"correct","setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["code"],"must":["slow","fast"],"askMustNot":["fast->next->next","slow == fast"]}
@@ tutor
A singly linked list is a chain of nodes: each holds a value and a pointer to the next node, and the last one points to NULL. Walking the list means following `next` until NULL.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

struct Node { int value; struct Node *next; };

void print_list(const struct Node *n) {
    for (; n; n = n->next) printf("%d%s", n->value, n->next ? " " : "\n");
}

int main(void) {
    struct Node c = {3, NULL}, b = {2, &c}, a = {1, &b};
    print_list(&a);
    return 0;
}
```

```
1 2 3
```

<solution>Keep prev = NULL. While head is not NULL: save next = head->next, set head->next = prev, then prev = head and head = next. Return prev. The program prints 1 2 3 and then 3 2 1.</solution>

<ask type="code" lang="c">Add `struct Node *reverse(struct Node *head)` that reverses the list in place by re-pointing the `next` fields and returns the new head. Print the list before and after.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
```c
#include <stdio.h>

struct Node { int value; struct Node *next; };

void print_list(const struct Node *n) {
    for (; n; n = n->next) printf("%d%s", n->value, n->next ? " " : "\n");
}

struct Node *reverse(struct Node *head) {
    struct Node *prev = NULL;
    while (head) {
        struct Node *next = head->next;
        head->next = prev;
        prev = head;
        head = next;
    }
    return prev;
}

int main(void) {
    struct Node c = {3, NULL}, b = {2, &c}, a = {1, &b};
    print_list(&a);
    print_list(reverse(&a));
    return 0;
}
```
This prints 1 2 3 and then 3 2 1. I had it wrong at first because I overwrote head->next before I'd kept a copy of it and lost the rest of the list, so I added the next variable to hold on to it.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">`struct Node *next = head->next;` before `head->next = prev;` is the step that matters, for the reason you found, and the program prints 1 2 3 then 3 2 1. It is one pass, $O(n)$ time, with three pointers of extra space.</grade>

Next, a list that never ends. If the last node points back into the list there is no NULL, and `print_list` would loop for ever. Floyd's method detects this with two pointers that start at the head: `slow` moves one node per step and `fast` moves two. With no cycle, `fast` runs off the end. With a cycle, both end up going round it, the gap between them shrinks by one node each step, and they meet.

<note>Floyd's cycle detection: advance `slow` by one node and `fast` by two. `fast` reaching NULL means no cycle; `slow` and `fast` pointing at the same node means a cycle. $O(n)$ time, $O(1)$ space.</note>

<solution>int has_cycle(const struct Node *head) { const struct Node *slow = head, *fast = head; while (fast && fast->next) { slow = slow->next; fast = fast->next->next; if (slow == fast) return 1; } return 0; } It prints 0 for the plain list and 1 after c.next = &b.</solution>

<ask type="code" lang="c">Write `int has_cycle(const struct Node *head)` with a slow and a fast pointer. In main, build the list 1, 2, 3 again and print `has_cycle` for it, then set `c.next = &b` and print it once more.</ask>
@@ verify
out, err, cc, code = run_c_full(block(learner, 'c'), 'c')
assert out == '1 2 3\n3 2 1\n' and code == 0 and not cc.strip(), (out, err, cc)
assert run_c(block(tutor[0], 'c')) == '1 2 3\n'
ref = r'''
#include <stdio.h>
struct Node { int value; struct Node *next; };
int has_cycle(const struct Node *head) {
    const struct Node *slow = head, *fast = head;
    while (fast && fast->next) {
        slow = slow->next;
        fast = fast->next->next;
        if (slow == fast) return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
int main(void) {
    struct Node c = {3, NULL}, b = {2, &c}, a = {1, &b};
    printf("%d\n", has_cycle(&a));
    c.next = &b;
    printf("%d\n", has_cycle(&a));
    struct Node one = {9, NULL};
    printf("%d %d\n", has_cycle(&one), has_cycle(NULL));
    one.next = &one;
    printf("%d\n", has_cycle(&one));
    return 0;
}
'''
assert run_c(ref).split() == ['0', '1', '0', '0', '1']
# Floyd's method against a visited-set check on every functional graph shape up to 7 nodes
def floyd(nxt, head):
    slow = fast = head
    while fast is not None and nxt[fast] is not None:
        slow = nxt[slow]; fast = nxt[nxt[fast]]
        if slow == fast: return True
    return False
for n in range(1, 8):
    for back in [None] + list(range(n)):
        nxt = {i: i + 1 for i in range(n - 1)}; nxt[n - 1] = back
        assert floyd(nxt, 0) == (back is not None)
assert 'fast->next->next' in solution(reply) and 'fast->next->next' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ hands-on-python-queue-two-stacks-pours-always
situation: hands-on
topic: A queue from two stacks
options: {"strategy":"code"}
voice: overconfident
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"partial","accept":["wrong"],"names":["every pop|each pop|on every","outbox"],"must":["1 3 2"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["code"],"askMustNot":["if not self\\.outbox"]}
@@ tutor
A stack gives back the most recent item first. A Python list is one, with `append` and `pop`:

```python
s = []
for x in (1, 2, 3):
    s.append(x)
print(s.pop(), s.pop(), s.pop())
```

```
3 2 1
```

Pouring one stack into another reverses the order again, and that is how two stacks can make a first-in, first-out queue.

<solution>push appends to inbox. pop: if outbox is empty, pour all of inbox into it (pop from inbox, append to outbox); then pop from outbox. The pour happens only when outbox is empty.</solution>
<check lang="python">
class Queue:
    def __init__(self): self.inbox, self.outbox = [], []
    def push(self, x): self.inbox.append(x)
    def pop(self):
        if not self.outbox:
            while self.inbox: self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())
        return self.outbox.pop()
q = Queue(); q.push(1); q.push(2); a = q.pop(); q.push(3)
assert (a, q.pop(), q.pop()) == (1, 2, 3)
</check>

<ask type="code" lang="python">Write a class `Queue` with `push(x)` and `pop()` that stores its items in two lists used only as stacks, and returns items in the order they were pushed. Show it working.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
```python
class Queue:
    def __init__(self):
        self.inbox, self.outbox = [], []
    def push(self, x):
        self.inbox.append(x)
    def pop(self):
        while self.inbox:
            self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())
        return self.outbox.pop()

q = Queue()
q.push(1); q.push(2); q.push(3)
print(q.pop(), q.pop(), q.pop())
```
Prints 1 2 3. Done, that's FIFO from two stacks. Pretty simple once you see the pour trick.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.5">The pour itself, `self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())`, is right, and your test does print 1 2 3. The fault is when it pours: `while self.inbox:` runs on every pop, even when `outbox` still holds older items, so a newer item lands on top of them and comes out first.</grade>

Your test pushes everything before popping anything, so it cannot show this. Here is your class with a push between two pops:

```python
class Queue:
    def __init__(self):
        self.inbox, self.outbox = [], []
    def push(self, x):
        self.inbox.append(x)
    def pop(self):
        while self.inbox:
            self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())
        return self.outbox.pop()

q = Queue()
q.push(1); q.push(2)
first = q.pop()
q.push(3)
print(first, q.pop(), q.pop())
```

```
1 3 2
```

After the first pop, `outbox` holds 2. The second pop pours 3 on top of it and returns 3.

<card>In a queue built from two stacks, when may pop pour the inbox into the outbox? :: Only when the outbox is empty. Pouring onto a non-empty outbox puts newer items above older ones and breaks the FIFO order.</card>

<solution>Guard the pour: `if not self.outbox:` before the while loop. The interleaved test then prints 1 2 3.</solution>
<check lang="python">
class Queue:
    def __init__(self): self.inbox, self.outbox = [], []
    def push(self, x): self.inbox.append(x)
    def pop(self):
        if not self.outbox:
            while self.inbox: self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())
        return self.outbox.pop()
q = Queue(); q.push(1); q.push(2); first = q.pop(); q.push(3)
assert (first, q.pop(), q.pop()) == (1, 2, 3)
</check>

<ask type="code" lang="python">Change `pop` so that the order survives a push between pops, and run the interleaved test above again.</ask>
